{"proposal":{"arguments":[],"assumptions":[{"assumption_id":"node.2249d844381920c4bb3ae2cf.a0","provenance_refs":[],"statement":"Footnote 5's unqualified direction to confirm and demonstrate creates a minimum demonstration duty independently of a request.","status":"PROVISIONAL"},{"assumption_id":"node.2249d844381920c4bb3ae2cf.a1","provenance_refs":[],"statement":"Paragraph 14's request qualification continues to govern its broader or specifically requested demonstration; this reconciliation is uncertain.","status":"PROVISIONAL"}],"bundle_hash":"efcec0609a15c3a67dd39baa7c24fcae46bddd7c2dcab5c0faca8a120e8c1dfd","controlled_language":"[TRUE_IS_SATISFIED] For an in-scope Product Provider, true means a minimum demonstration is called for under paragraph 14 and footnote 5 regardless of whether a request exists; false does not arise within this reading's scope. This is an obligation-activation predicate, not a finding of fulfillment. Footnote 5 independently requires confirmation and demonstration to the SFC of compliance with paragraphs 10–13 and the specified smart-contract assurance. Read together with paragraph 14, SFC satisfaction plausibly governs the demonstration, while the reach of high confidence remains unresolved. Without a request, this minimum duty persists; paragraphs 15 and 16 do not activate their respective requirements to obtain third-party audit or verification and a satisfactory legal opinion.","family_ids":["dependencies"],"parent_id":null,"revision_reason":"On this rival reading, no request is needed for the footnote's minimum demonstration. Its strongest weakness is that it reduces the apparent effect of paragraph 14's express request qualification. Footnote 5 does not similarly remove the independent request conditions in paragraphs 15 and 16.","source_packet_hash":"d74180e126dda06342060a17b37c3227fc695e9fc0f0d5ce68b187d7cb8bc612","source_unit_ids":["dc689bdf0ffcf.u3.944","dc689bdf0ffcf.u4.1185","dc689bdf0ffcf.u5.1464","dc689bdf0ffcf.u6.1694"],"su